# A FOUR-BAR LINKAGE (M25).
#
# The M25 gate: drive one link, and the other three follow correctly.
#
# This is the thing M24 could not do and said so by name. A tree of mates is
# solved exactly, one step at a time, outwards from the ground -- but a four-bar
# is a CLOSED LOOP, and a loop has no first step. M24 refused it rather than
# approximating, because an approximate linkage is one that does not close, and
# it looks perfectly convincing in a picture.
#
# M25 buys the iterative solver that closes it.
#
# Run it:
#   ep3d --script examples/four-bar.ep3ds

# --- one part, four sizes -----------------------------------------------------
#
# Every link is a bar with a pin hole at each end. The same script draws all
# four because they differ only in length -- and the two connectors are what
# make a bar a LINK: one connector at the near end, one at the far end, both
# axes pointing +Z so the pins are parallel and the linkage stays planar.
#
# THE NAMES CARRY THE LINK'S NAME because this script keeps ONE document -- each
# `save` writes everything drawn so far -- and a connector name has to be unique
# in a document. It has to be, because a mate names its ends by NAME: two
# connectors called "A" would make every mate mean whichever came first. That
# is refused now; it used to be accepted, and this example is what found it.

sketch GroundBar
tool rect
click 0 -4
click 100 4
pad Ground 6
connector GroundA 0 0 0 0 0 1
connector GroundB 100 0 0 0 0 1
solve
save build/fourbar-ground.ep3d

sketch CrankBar
tool rect
click 0 -4
click 30 4
pad Crank 6
connector CrankA 0 0 0 0 0 1
connector CrankB 30 0 0 0 0 1
solve
save build/fourbar-crank.ep3d

sketch CouplerBar
tool rect
click 0 -4
click 110 4
pad Coupler 6
connector CouplerA 0 0 0 0 0 1
connector CouplerB 110 0 0 0 0 1
solve
save build/fourbar-coupler.ep3d

sketch RockerBar
tool rect
click 0 -4
click 60 4
pad Rocker 6
connector RockerA 0 0 0 0 0 1
connector RockerB 60 0 0 0 0 1
solve
save build/fourbar-rocker.ep3d

# --- the linkage --------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Lengths 100, 30, 110, 60. That is a GRASHOF crank-rocker: the shortest link
# plus the longest (30 + 110 = 140) is less than the other two (100 + 60 = 160),
# and the shortest is next to the ground -- so the crank goes all the way round
# and the rocker swings back and forth. Pick the lengths wrong and "turn it a
# full circle" is a demand the mechanism cannot meet.

assembly FourBar

insert Ground build/fourbar-ground.ep3d Ground
insert Crank build/fourbar-crank.ep3d Crank
insert Coupler build/fourbar-coupler.ep3d Coupler
insert Rocker build/fourbar-rocker.ep3d Rocker

ground Ground

# Four pins, in a ring: Ground.A - Crank.A, Crank.B - Coupler.A,
# Coupler.B - Rocker.A, Rocker.B - Ground.B. The fourth one closes the loop.
mate revolute J1 Ground/GroundA Crank/CrankA 0
mate revolute J2 Crank/CrankB Coupler/CouplerA
mate revolute J3 Coupler/CouplerB Rocker/RockerA
mate revolute J4 Rocker/RockerB Ground/GroundB

# THE CRANK IS THE ONE THE USER TURNS. Everything else is what the solve is
# allowed to move -- and saying which is which is the whole difference between
# a mechanism and a contradiction: four revolutes with four fixed angles is
# almost never a closed loop.
drive J1 0

echo --- the linkage, crank at 0 ---
solve
measure

# --- and now turn the crank ---------------------------------------------------
#
# One number changes. The coupler and the rocker go to angles nobody typed, and
# every one of the four pins stays a pin.
#
# The rocker's centre of mass is what to watch: it swings back and forth while
# the crank goes round, which is what a crank-rocker IS.

drive J1 60
echo --- crank at 60 ---
solve
measure

drive J1 120
echo --- crank at 120 ---
solve
measure

drive J1 240
echo --- crank at 240 ---
solve
measure

# --- limits and interference ---------------------------------------------------
#
# A limit STOPS a motion rather than refusing it (roadmap section 22): dragging
# past a stop should stop, not error. But the stop is said out loud -- a control
# that silently ignores what it was told is a control that appears broken.

limit J1 0 90
drive J1 200
echo --- driven past the stop ---
solve
measure

limit J1 off

# Interference is SEPARATE from mates, because a perfectly legal set of mates
# can still drive two parts through each other. Bounding boxes first, then a
# real intersection on whatever survives that.
drive J1 90
solve
echo --- does anything collide? ---
interference

save build/four-bar.ep3da
